pervisors with the study’s aims and sought oral consent to participate. Consent was monitored throughout the period of study as the participants kept voluntarily submitting their feedback forms to the researchers. All data were handled strictly confidentially and anonymised before analysis. References 1. Liao JM, Wachter RM. Grading residents'' clinical performance: unique opportunities and challenges. J Gen Intern Med 2014;29:983–5. 10.1007/s11606-014-2856-3 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Frank JR, Snell LS, Cate OT, et al. Competency-based medical education: theory to practice. Medical Teacher 2010;32:638–45. 10.3109/0142159X.2010.501190 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Renting N, Dornan T, Gans ROB, et al. What supervisors say in their feedback: construction of Canmeds roles in workplace settings. Adv in Health Sci Educ 2016;21:375–87. 10.1007/s10459-015-9634-9 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Ginsburg S, Gold W, Cavalcanti RB, et al. “Competencies "plus": the nature of written comments on internal medicine residents'' evaluation forms”. Acad Med 2011;86:S30–4. 10.1097/ACM.0b013e31822a6d92 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Lurie SJ, Mooney CJ, Lyness JM. Pitfalls in assessment of competency-based educational objectives. Acad Med 2011;86:412–4. 10.1097/ACM.0b013e31820cdb28 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Leslie K, Baker L, Egan-Lee E, et al. Advancing faculty development in medical education: a systematic review. Acad Med 2013;88:1038–45. 10.1097/ACM.0b013e318294fd29 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Holmboe ES, Ward DS, Reznick RK, et al. Faculty development in assessment: the missing link in competency-based medical education. Acad Med 2011;86:460–7. 10.1097/ACM.0b013e31820cb2a7 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Cole KA, Barker LR, Kolodner K, et al. Faculty development in teaching skills: an intensive longitudinal model. Acad Med 2004;79:469–80. 10.1097/00001888-20