INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(2511,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10092236','Patient Unique Stigmatization Holistic tool in dermatology (PUSH-D): Development and validation of a dermatology-specific stigmatization assessment tool.','Ezzedine K, Shourick J, Bergqvist C, Misery L, Chuberre B, Kerob D, Halioua B, Le Fur G, Paul C, Richard MA, Taieb C.','2022','rses rses vsd disgust vsd vsd disapproval devalued prejudices stigmatizing vsd vsd vsd vsd methodologist sociologist vsd isdl sociologist polling conseil quota morphea wrinkles slackening promax olkin kmo eigenvalues cattell scree inflexion eigenvalue tli rses rses henri mondor mandarin ispor grenoble se5 a01880 morphea kmo unidimensionality pushd ruining outings hairdresser masseur manicure conceal q14 q15 q16 q17 q18 q19 stared strangers q20 q21 q22 unidimensionality rses stigmatizing vsd podoconiosis isdl vsd rses vsd chuberre delphine kerob oreal khaled ezzedine shourick bergqvist misery halioua gaelle carle aleth taieb ezzedine shourick bergqvist misery chuberre kerob se5 a01880 khaled ezzedine khaled ezzedine aphp taieb taieb maymone karimkhani rundle tizek schielein biedermann bohner unreferred oktoberfest creadore manjaly tkachenko takeshita stigmatizing laypersons fino mazzetti brakel cataldo kohrt nyblade stockton shenefelt shenefelt goffman spoiled germain legau bogoeva desroches scambler illn roosta davern sufferers e0205009 topp andrees mrowietz mrowietz sikorski kiedrowski schlette radtke destigmatization sikorski roßmann topp mokkink terwee knol stratford seidenberg haltiner wyler leidy revicki geneste dimitrov matusiak jafferany szepietowski awm duller kerkhof valk gerritsen isdl warrington speeding psychometrika rse cond eremenco mcelroy verjee ispor rmh dadun brakel zweekhorst mbm damayanti bunders jfg e3274 rensen bandyopadhyay brakel brakel mutatkar bakirtzief stevelink hoekstra nardi smt banstola premkumar fauerbach heinberg thombs scq heinberg fauerbach heinberg kudel medley wigley tora deribe reda podoconiosis burchard niederauer lamprecht kunsebeck venerol verwandte geb dimitrov szepietowski postepy hig dosw siddhi mcgrouther bayat jamrozik sarwer disfigurement','Abstract Background The skin plays an important role in establishing interpersonal relationships, and thus visible skin disorders, which have a significant impact on physical appearance, influence other people''s behaviours and attitudes. Objective To develop and validate a dermatologic‐specific questionnaire to evaluate stigmatization in individuals with visible skin conditions. Methods Items were generated by a verbatim report based on qualitative interviews with patients with various dermatologic conditions. Subsequently, a study was implemented for psychometric analysis. A dermatology‐specific stigmatization questionnaire (PUSH‐D) was refined via item reduction according to inter‐question correlations, consensus among experts and exploratory factor analysis. Internal consistency was determined by calculating Cronbach''s α. Concurrent validity was determined by calculating the correlation between PUSH‐D and the Dermatology Life Quality Index (DLQI) and the Rosenberg Self‐Esteem Scale (RSES). Results From a primary list of 22 items, PUSH‐D was reduced to a 17‐item questionnaire, covering two pertinent dimensions based on the exploratory factor analysis. Construct validity was demonstrated, and PUSH‐D showed good internal consistency (Cronbach''s α = 0.9). PUSH‐D correlated strongly with the DLQI 0.72 ( p < 0.001) and moderately with the RSES 0.49 ( p < 0.001). Conclusion PUSH‐D allows a comprehensive view of the degree of stigmatization in visible skin disorders, as well as the comparability of stigmatization levels across various skin conditions. INTRODUCTION While skin diseases are often dismissed as a cosmetic issue, they affect a quarter of the population and constitute a major cause of disability worldwide. 1 , 2 Being the largest and most visible organ of the human body, the skin communicates valuable information about a person''s health. The skin plays an important role in establishing interpersonal relationships, and thus visible skin disorders (VSD), which have a significant impact on physical appearance, influence other people''s behaviours and attitudes. 3 , 4 Any change in skin colour or appearance can potentially be a cause of discrimination, adding to the physical burden of disease. 5 , 6 Visibility of skin changes can elicit disgust or fear of contagion, further contributing to the social exclusion and isolation of patients suffering from VSD. Moreover, the spiritual and religious aspects of skin disorders have been recognized since ancient times and have often resulted in widespread and long‐lasting misconceptions about VSD. 7 All these elements are the characteristics of stigma, which is classically defined as the extreme disapproval of an individual based on an attribute that is perceived to distinguish them from other members of a society. 8 Stigma is a multi‐faceted construct and its conceptual models have progressed over time. 6 , 9 These models have coined several terms such as public (or external) stigma, self‐stigma, felt‐stigma and enacted‐stigma, among others. Public stigma (or external) refers to the stigmatization and discrimination against a group of people with devalued characteristics, whereas self‐stigma occurs due to the internalization of these stereotypes and prejudices. 9 Enacted stigma refers to the perceived act of discrimination against individuals with a stigmatizing condition, whereas felt‐stigma refers to the individual''s feelings of embarrassment and shame associated with the condition as well as the anticipation and expectation of discrimination or negative treatment by others. 10 The psychosocial sequelae of stigmatization in skin diseases—such as acne, vitiligo, psoriasis and atopic dermatitis—are well recognized. 11 , 12 Increased awareness on the impact of stigma has prompted efforts to implement effective stigma‐reduction strategies. 13 , 14 , 15 In order to capture the impact and degree of stigmatization in individuals with VSD, valid and reliable assessment tools are needed. A recent systematic review on patient reported outcome measures (PROM) assessing stigma in VSD concluded that 12 instruments could be recommended for use; however, none of these tools are specific for dermatology. 16 The purpose of the current study was therefore to develop and validate a dermatologic‐specific questionnaire to evaluate stigmatization in individuals with VSD. METHODS A step‐by‐step approach was used to create a questionnaire that measures stigma in patients with VSD (Figure S1 ). This study followed the COSMIN recommendations on outcome measurement instruments. 17 This self‐administered questionnaire was established using three standardized phases, namely the ‘exploratory,’ ‘development’ and ‘validation’ phases. 18 , 19 A multidisciplinary working group developed and validated the questionnaire; it was comprised of experts in questionnaire design and development, PROM experts, a methodologist, healthcare professionals including two dermatologists and one psychologist, a sociologist and patients'' associations. Step 1: Elaboration of the questionnaire A literature search was conducted on PubMed looking for published questionnaires or scoring systems related to stigmatization in dermatology. We found a recent systematic review on PROM assessing stigma in VSD. Two questionnaires, the 6‐item Stigmatization Scale 20 and the Impact of Chronic Skin Disease on Daily Life (ISDL) Stigma sub‐scale, 21 are dermatology‐specific; however, they were not recommended for use as they both lack structural validity, and their methodology was qualified as inadequate. 16 Furthermore, among dermatology specific quality of life scales, several instruments included few items related to stigmatization. The next step involved the creation of a verbatim report based on qualitative interviews between a dermatologist, a sociologist, patients'' associations, and patients with various dermatologic conditions. Patients were enrolled consecutively from those who consulted a hospital consultation or a community‐based private general dermatology consultation and who had given their consent to participate in the study ( n = 26). Their responses were analysed by two researchers with experience in qualitative studies and led to the identification of two important themes: patient perception and feelings. The final choice of questions was made by the working group. On the basis of this report, the working group created a list of 22 items that were converted into questions with answers using a 5‐point Likert scale: ‘never’ (rated 0), ‘rarely’ (1), ‘sometimes’ (2), ‘often’ (3), ‘very often’ (4). Another response ‘Not applicable’ was included to limit missing data and was rated 0. The latter helps avoid missing data as much as possible and allows the respondent the opportunity to express his or her feelings. Each item was introduced by the sentence ‘During the last month’ to define a precise time frame and limit memory bias. Step 2: Development phase A polling institute (HC Conseil Paris, France) was then asked to distribute the questionnaire through online surveys to a representative sample of the French adult general population aged more than 18 years recruited using a stratified proportional quota sampling with replacement design and who declared 1/having been diagnosed by a physician for one of the following skin disease acne, atopic dermatitis, rosacea, psoriasis, vitiligo, hidradenitis suppurativa, alopecia areata and morphea or 2/declaring having a facial scarring and/or skin with aging signs (wrinkles, slackening, abnormal pigmentation). The survey was conducted between January and July 2021. Step 3: Measurement properties of the questionnaire The measurement properties of the questionnaire were assessed in four steps: (i) reduction of the number of items, (ii) subscale repartition of items and assessment of factorial validity, (iii) assessment of construct validity and (iv) assessment of reliability. Reduction of the number of items We reduced the number of items based on redundancy, which was suspected when the inter‐item correlation evaluated by the Spearman correlation coefficient was above 0.8. Subscale repartition of items and assessment of factorial validity An exploratory factor analysis with promax rotation was then conducted on the remaining items. Suitability for factorial analysis was assessed by a Kaiser‐Meyer‐Olkin (KMO) value above 0.8 for all items. To define the number of factors to retain, we used Parallel Analysis (PA; PA implies a Monte Carlo simulation process since ‘expected’ Eigenvalues are obtained by simulating normal random samples that parallel the observed data in terms of sample size and number of variables) combined with Cattell''s Scree Test (which identifies the inflexion point on the Eigenvalue graph). Factor loadings ≥0.50 were considered as significant and thus were retained. Items were considered for deletion if they loaded on two or more factors or did not load on any factors. Data analysis was conducted using R program (version 4.0.3), with the significance level set at 0.05. To evaluate the questionnaire''s internal consistency and confirm its reliability, the item homogeneity in each factor was tested using Cronbach''s alpha coefficient, aiming to have both >0.7 for the entire questionnaire and each subscale. 22 Factorial validity was assessed using a higher‐order factor confirmatory analysis. This analysis confirmed that the subscales could be combined into a single score. The model''s suitability was assessed using two criteria: comparative fit index (CFI) above 0.9, Tucker–Lewis index (TLI) above 0.9 and root mean square error of approximation (RMSEA) under 0.08. Construct validity To assess the questionnaire''s external validity, all participants were asked to complete two previously validated self‐administered questionnaires: the Dermatology Life Quality Index [DLQI] questionnaire and the Rosenberg Self‐Esteem Scale. The DLQI questionnaire is the first dermatology‐specific instrument designed to assess the impact of skin diseases on patient QoL. 23 , 24 It is a simple, practical and validated questionnaire that grades QoL designed for adults and patients aged over 16 years. The DLQI score being the sum of all scores (0–30), with high scores reflecting poor QoL. Rosenberg''s self‐esteem scale (RSES) was developed as a measure of subjective self‐evaluation in 1965 as a 10‐question Likert‐type scale. 25 This measure is widely used in social‐science research, especially within the fields of sociology and psychology. The scale comprises of five positive and five negative statements. The scale ranges from 0 to 30. The higher the score, the higher the self‐esteem. Concurrent validity was established by calculating Pearson correlations between the stigmatization questionnaire and the DLQI and RSES validated questionnaires. A univariate linear regression analysis was also performed to assess the relationship between the scores of stigmatization questionnaire and self‐reported clinical severity. The respondents evaluated disease severity on a visual analogue scale between 1 and 10, with 1 being low severity and 10 very high severity. The subjects were divided into three groups, namely low severity (scores of 1–3), moderate severity (4–6) and high severity (7–10). Reliability Test–retest analyses were carried out to assess reproducibility. Consecutive participants attending the department of dermatology of Henri Mondor University hospital between June 1, 2021, and December 1, 2021, and who were diagnosed as having acne, vitiligo, psoriasis, atopic dermatitis or alopecia areata were asked to participate to the study. Patients who gave their oral consent answered the questionnaire twice with a 10‐day interval in‐between. Reliability between the two assessments was measured by the intraclass correlation coefficient ICC for agreement. 26 Agreement was represented by Bland and Altman plots. 27 Step 4: Translation, cross‐cultural adaptation and cognitive debriefing The validated methodology was applied to generate a US English, Mandarin Chinese, German, Italian, Brazilian Portuguese and Spanish‐language versions according to the recommendations of the ISPOR task force. 28 This process comprises a 9‐step procedure in order to refine the translation while taking into account subtle nuances of the source document. Ethical considerations This project obtained a favourable opinion from the Ethics Committee of the CHU Grenoble Paris (CCP SE5 ‐ Sud‐Est V‐ 2019‐A01880‐57) on 22 October, 2019, and conducted according to the Declaration of Helsinki. According to French regulations for this type of research (Reference Methodology 003), no written informed consent had to be obtained from the participating patients, and they were provided with an information and non‐objection note. RESULTS Exploratory phase A total of 26 patients (16 women, 10 men; ages 16–77 years) with various skin diseases including acne, atopic dermatitis, psoriasis, vitiligo, hidradenitis suppurativa, alopecia areata and morphea were included. Interviews were conducted between November 2020 and April 2021. They included closed‐ended questions regarding social demographics and skin condition information; as well as open‐ended questions allowing unrestricted answers. Textual analysis of these interviews led to the identification of 34 items grouped around two themes: patient perception and feelings. The original 34 items were then reduced to 22 questions by the working group to limit redundancy. Development and validation phase Study population A total of 2620 participants responded to the questionnaire. Their mean age was 45.14 years ± 15.59; 1494 were women (57%) and 1126 were men (43%). Patient characteristics are described in Table 1 . TABLE 1. Socio‐demographic and clinical characteristics of patients included in the validation step Mean (SD) or N (%) Sex Man 1126 (43%) Woman 1494 (57%) Age 45.1 ± 15.6 Self‐reported disease severity N % Low 1123 42.9% Moderate 1176 44.9% High 253 9.7% Skin condition 2620 Atopic dermatitis/eczema 501 Psoriasis 501 Acne 500 Rosacea 150 Hair loss 261 Skin aging 304 Visible facial scar 153 Vitiligo 250 Open in a new tab Reduction of the number of items First of all, exploratory factor analysis was conducted in all participants to test the robustness of the global 22‐item questionnaire. Five items were then deleted because of nondiscrimination. Subscale repartition of items and assessment of factorial validity All KMO scores were above 0.90, which confirmed the compatibility of the items for factorial analysis. The screen plot determined two pertinent dimensions (Figure 1 ). These were related to ‘felt‐stigma’ (8 items) and ‘enacted stigma’ (9 items). No item was eliminated as none had a low factor loading or a high cross factor loading. Our final questionnaire therefore included 17 items (Table 2 ). The confirmatory factor analysis diagram is found in Figure 1 . FIGURE 1. Open in a new tab Confirmation of the unidimensionality of PUSHD using was a higher order factor analysis. Tucker–Lewis index = 0.968, RMSEA = 0.053, 95% Confidence Interval [0.05–0.056]), comparative fit index (CFI) = 0.976. TABLE 2. Exploratory factor analysis with standardized regression coefficients from the final rotated factor pattern Question Felt‐Stigma Enacted Stigma Over the last 4 weeks, because of the way others look at you, did you feel that it was better to… Q1 …avoid appearing in family photos for fear of ruining them 0.08 0.65 Q2 …avoid some people 0.07 0.72 Q3 denied accompanying your relatives (children/partners) on public outings 0.46 0.37 Q4 …not use a professional for body care (hairdresser, masseur, manicure) 0.20 0.55 Q5 …not approaching other people spontaneously −0.03 0.83 Q6 …refuse direct contact with the public 0.14 0.69 Q7 …avoid being put in the spotlight (physically) −0.16 0.90 Q8 be careful about the clothes you wear 0.2 0.34 Q9 stay at home to avoid being seen 0.39 0.37 Q10 …conceal/hide the visible parts of your affected skin −0.05 0.69 Q11 …not show yourself, hide yourself 0.06 0.77 Over the last 4 weeks, because of the way others look at you, have you felt… Q12 …that you have been less loved [appreciated] by your family, your friends 0.62 0.16 Q13 …that you brought shame to your family, your relatives 0.69 0.14 Q14 feel alone in your own family 0.45 0.34 Q15 …that you were pushed away by your partner 0.75 0.04 Q16 …that people avoided shaking your hand 0.85 −0.04 Q17 …that you have been treated differently during administrative procedures 0.82 0.00 Q18 …that some people view you as dirty 0.79 0.01 Q19 being stared at in the street by strangers 0.39 0.33 Q20 …that you have faced discrimination at work 0.85 −0.06 Q21 …that you have been left out by your colleagues at work 0.87 −0.05 Q22 …that people have avoided sitting next to you on public transportation, and in public places 0.84 −0.01 Open in a new tab Items shown in bold are those retained in the dimension based on the best discrimination factor. Only items with discrimination factor higher than 0.4 are considered. The unidimensionality of the questionnaire was confirmed by a higher order factor analysis. The Tucker–Lewis index was 0.968 (acceptable if greater than 0.9), the RMSEA (0.053, CI 95% [0.05–0.056]; acceptable if lower than 0.08) and comparative fit index (CFI) above 0.9 was (0.976), Cronbach''s alpha coefficient was 0.945 for the entire questionnaire, 0.942 for ‘felt‐stigma’ subscale and 0.911 for ‘enacted stigma’ subscale, reflecting its excellent internal coherence. Construct validity The 17‐item questionnaire correlated strongly with the DLQI 0.72 ( p < 0.001) and moderately with the RSES 0.49 ( p < 0.001). Self‐reported clinical severity of skin condition was available for 1502 patients. Patients reporting a high severity were 12.34 times more likely to have a higher stigmatization score compared with patients reporting a low severity (Table 3 ). TABLE 3. Univariate linear regression with ‘mild severity’ as the reference category Self‐reported skin condition severity Total score Felt‐stigma Enacted stigma Estimates 95% CI P value 95% CI P value 95% CI P value Moderate 4.34 2.45–6.23 <0.001 2.33 1.29–3.37 <0.001 2.01 0.99–3.03 <0.001 Severe 12.34 8.87–15.80 <0.001 6.99 5.08–8.91 <0.001 5.34 3.47–7.22 <0.001 Open in a new tab Abbreviations: CI, confidence interval; estimates, beta. Test–retest analysis The test–retest analysis was conducted on 28 patients. Reliability was good, with an ICC for agreement of 0.941 (95% CI [0.876–0.972]), 0.986 (95% CI [0.969–0.994]) and 0.963 (95% CI [0.918–0.983]), respectively, for felt‐stigma, enacted stigma and total score. The Bland and Altman diagram is presented in Figure 2 . FIGURE 2. Open in a new tab Bland and Altman plots for felt‐stigma, enacted stigma and total score. DISCUSSION We developed and validated a patient reported instrument, the Patient Unique Stigmatization Holistic tool in Dermatology (PUSH‐D) that assesses stigmatization in visible skin conditions. It measures two pertinent dimensions related to ‘felt‐stigma’ (8 items) and ‘enacted stigma’ (9 items). This report provides support for PUSH‐D''s feasibility, reliability and validity as a specific instrument designed to assess stigmatization in visible skin conditions. With its 17 items and six possible answers for each, this questionnaire is relatively short, understandable and easy‐to‐use by patients. PUSH‐D is a robust tool, as its internal consistency exceeds the minimum reliability criterion of 0.90. Our instrument complements existing measures of dermatologic quality of life such as the DLQI by addressing the gap in evaluating stigmatization in various dermatological conditions. Indeed, in most QOL questionnaires stigma is usually evaluated by one or two items in addition to other aspects of QOL. The interest of a stigma‐specific questionnaire is to evaluate, for example, whether a highly stigmatizing skin disease has a worse impact on certain aspects of life such as work productivity. Indeed, until today, there was no adequately validated instrument developed to assess stigmatization specifically in dermatology in such a large sample size. In a recent systematic review on PROM assessing stigma in VSD, 22 instruments were found, 12 of which were recommended for use (received A‐rating as per COSMIN guidelines on PROM). These instruments are disease‐specific as they were validated in patients with Hansen''s disease, 29 , 30 , 31 , 32 burn victims, 33 , 34 scleroderma, 35 podoconiosis, 36 psoriasis 37 , 38 and atopic dermatitis. 36 Two questionnaires, the 6‐item Stigmatization Scale 20 and the Impact of Chronic Skin Disease on Daily Life (ISDL) Stigma sub‐scale, 21 are dermatology‐specific; however, they both lack structural validity, and their methodology was qualified as inadequate and were not recommended for use. Dermatology specific instrument such as PUSH‐D is necessary to compare the stigmatization level across various skin conditions. 39 It can of course be further supplemented with a disease‐specific instrument for more in‐depth analysis of QOL and stigmatization. It is worth mentioning that PUSH‐D is a dermatology specific instrument which is not limited to visible dermatologic diseases only, but also includes visible skin conditions such as facial scars and skin aging which are both highly stigmatized. 40 , 41 Furthermore, the systematic review on PROM assessing stigma in VSD concluded that most existing instruments mainly measure felt‐stigma, whereas validated instruments measuring enacted stigma are lacking. 16 PUSH‐D covers both felt‐stigma and enacted stigma. PUSH‐D was also able to discriminate between levels of disease severity, supporting the tool''s discriminant validity. Consistent with our hypothesis, high PUSH‐D scores were associated with patients reporting high disease severity, whereas low PUSH‐D scores were linked to those reporting low disease activity. Moreover, PUSH‐D was shown to exhibit external validity, correlating well with the DLQI and RSES scales. An important strength to our study is the inclusion of a large number of participants ( n = 2620) with a large variety of skin conditions. Its main limitation is that it was conducted in France only. Thus, our results may not be generalizable to other cultural contexts. In conclusion, we have herein developed and validated a dermatologic‐specific stigmatization score, PUSH‐D. This score was tested in a large population with a variety of skin conditions. Its psychometric validity and reliability allow a comprehensive view of the degree of stigmatization in VSD, as well as the comparability of stigmatization levels across various skin conditions. This brings us one step closer to implementing interventions to reduce stigmatization in the general public. FUNDING INFORMATION This study received funding from Cosmetic Active International and Lilly France. CONFLICT OF INTEREST Bertrand Chuberre and Delphine Kerob are employed by L''Oréal. Khaled Ezzedine; Jason Shourick; Christina Bergqvist; Laurent Misery; Bruno Halioua; Gaëlle Le Fur; Carle Paul; Marie‐Aleth Richard and Charles Taieb have no conflict of interest to declare. Supporting information Figure S1 Click here for additional data file. (23.3KB, docx) ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS We acknowledge the active participation of all patients who made this study feasible. Ezzedine K, Shourick J, Bergqvist C, Misery L, Chuberre B, Kerob D, et al. Patient Unique Stigmatization Holistic tool in dermatology (PUSH‐D): Development and validation of a dermatology‐specific stigmatization assessment tool. J Eur Acad Dermatol Venereol. 2023;37:443–450. 10.1111/jdv.18641 IRB approval status: CCP SE5 ‐ Sud‐Est V‐ 2019‐A01880‐57. Contributor Information Kha