, “RadImageNet: An Open Radiologic Deep Learning Research Dataset for Effective Transfer Learning,” Radiology Artificial Intelligence 4, no. 5 (2022): 10315, 10.1148/ryai.210315. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 37. Haggenmüller S., Schmitt M., Krieghoff‐Henning E., et al., “Federated Learning for Decentralized Artificial Intelligence in Melanoma Diagnostics,” JAMA Dermatology 160, no. 3 (2024): 303–311, 10.1001/jamadermatol.2023.5550. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 38. Rieke N., Hancox J., Li W., et al., “The Future of Digital Health With Federated Learning,” Npj Digital Medicine 3, no. 1 (2020): 119, 10.1038/s41746-020-00323-1. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 39. Cho S. I., Navarrete‐Dechent C., Daneshjou R., et al., “Generation of a Melanoma and Nevus Data Set From Unstandardized Clinical Photographs on the Internet,” JAMA Dermatology 159, no. 11 (2023): 1223–1231, 10.1001/jamadermatol.2023.3521. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 40. Charalambides M., Flohr C., Bahadoran P., and Matin R. N., “New International Reporting Guidelines for Clinical Trials Evaluating Effectiveness of Artificial Intelligence Interventions in Dermatology: Strengthening the SPIRIT of Robust Trial Reporting,” British Journal of Dermatology 184, no. 3 (2021): 381–383, 10.1111/bjd.19616. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 41. Daneshjou R., Barata C., Betz‐Stablein B., et al., “Checklist for Evaluation of Image‐Based Artificial Intelligence Reports in Dermatology,” JAMA Dermatology 158, no. 1 (2022): 90–96, 10.1001/jamadermatol.2021.4915. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Data Availability Statement Data sharing is not applicable to this article as no new data were created or analyzed in this study.',3172,'2025-10-14 06:22